'Well! I've often seen a cat without a grin,' thought Alice; 'but a grin without a cat! It's the most curious thing I ever saw in my life!'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
She had not gone much farther before she came in sight of the house of the March Hare: she thought it must be the right house, because the chimneys were shaped like ears and the roof was thatched with fur. It was so large a house, that she did not like to go nearer till she had nibbled some more of the lefthand bit of mushroom, and raised herself to about two feet high: even then she walked up towards it rather timidly, saying to herself 'Suppose it should be raving mad after all! I almost wish I'd gone to see the Hatter instead!'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {\par\pard\hyphpar }{\page } {\b
Chapter} 7 {	
\~\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {\s2 \afs28
{\b
A Mad Tea-Party\par\pard\plain\hyphpar}\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
\~\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
There was a table set out under a tree in front of the house, and the March Hare and the Hatter were having tea at it: a Dormouse was sitting between them, fast asleep, and the other two were using it as a cushion, resting their elbows on it, and talking over its head. 'Very uncomfortable for the Dormouse,' thought Alice; 'only, as it's asleep, I suppose it doesn't mind.'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
The table was a large one, but the three were all crowded together at one corner of it: 'No room! No room!' they cried out when they saw Alice coming. 'There's PLENTY of room!' said Alice indignantly, and she sat down in a large arm-chair at one end of the table.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Have some wine,' the March Hare said in an encouraging tone.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
Alice looked all round the table, but there was nothing on it but tea.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'I don't see any wine,' she remarked.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'There isn't any,' said the March Hare.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Then it wasn't very civil of you to offer it,' said Alice angrily.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'It wasn't very civil of you to sit down without being invited,' said the March Hare.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'I didn't know it was YOUR table,' said Alice; 'it's laid for a great many more than three.'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Your hair wants cutting,' said the Hatter. He had been looking at Alice for some time with great curiosity, and this was his first speech.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'You should learn not to make personal remarks,' Alice said with some severity; 'it's very rude.'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
The Hatter opened his eyes very wide on hearing this; but all he SAID was, 'Why is a raven like a writing-desk?'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Come, we shall have some fun now!' thought Alice. 'I'm glad they've begun asking riddles.--I believe I can guess that,' she added aloud.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Do you mean that you think you can find out the answer to it?' said the March Hare.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Exactly so,' said Alice.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Then you should say what you mean,' the March Hare went on.\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'I do,' Alice hastily replied; 'at least--at least I mean what I say--that's the same thing, you know.'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'Not the same thing a bit!' said the Hatter. 'You might just as well say that "I see what I eat" is the same thing as "I eat what I see"!'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'You might just as well say,' added the March Hare, 'that "I like what I get" is the same thing as "I get what I like"!'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
'You might just as well say,' added the Dormouse, who seemed to be talking in his sleep, 'that "I breathe when I sleep" is the same thing as "I sleep when I breathe"!'\par\pard\plain\hyphpar} {	
